  ]},
 {"clause_id": "c023",
  "issues": [
   {"issue": "The continuous-development sentence is self-contradictory on its face: \"at Farmee's further option, it shall continuously develop the Farmout Lands by commencing ... a minimum of two (2) Subsequent Wells per calendar year\" makes the same act simultaneously optional and mandatory. \"Fully developed\" is also left without any measure.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.62},
   {"issue": "Section 13(A) treats this subsection as a source of automatic termination \"as a consequence of Farmee's failure to meet the drilling commitments\", yet 3(B)(3) contains no termination language and casts continuous development as Farmee's option. The two provisions cannot both be right about whether annual drilling is a commitment.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.45}
  ]},
 {"clause_id": "c024",
  "issues": [
   {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.12},
   {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.09}
  ]},
 {"clause_id": "c025",
  "issues": [
   {"issue": "The trigger — a well \"lost for any reason\" or a \"mechanical difficulty or formation or condition which would render further drilling impractical or impossible\" — turns on the vague and largely self-judged word \"impractical\". Its boundary would have to be fixed by a court.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.31},
   {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
  ]},
 {"clause_id": "c026",
  "issues": [
   {"issue": "Delivery of the assignment is due only \"within sixty (60) days after Farmor is satisfied that Farmee has complied with all its obligations\", making the trigger Farmor's own subjective satisfaction with no standard, deadline or reasonableness qualifier. A court would have to decide whether that satisfaction may be withheld at will.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.46},
   {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
  ]},
 {"clause_id": "c027",
  "issues": [
   {"issue": "Forfeiture runs from sixty days after a well \"has been completed as a well capable of production in paying quantities\", but nothing fixes when that state is reached or who determines it. The date on which the forfeiture clock starts is therefore indeterminate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.32},
   {"issue": "Section 3(D)(1) makes Farmor's delivery obligation depend on Farmor's satisfaction after receiving Farmee's request, while this subsection forfeits the right if the request is late; the two clocks are measured from different, unsynchronised events. Reconciling the request deadline with the satisfaction condition requires construction.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
 {"clause_id": "c028",
  "issues": [
   {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.13},
   {"issue": "This subsection measures earned depths by footage (surface to 100' below total depth, capped at 4,000'), while the Exhibit \"D\" assignment reserves everything \"below the ____ formation\" — a stratigraphic limit, with the formation left blank. The two measures of the vertical grant do not match.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.34}
  ]},
 {"clause_id": "c029",
  "issues": [
   {"issue": "The provision runs two inconsistent regimes together: drilling failures terminate the Agreement automatically, while defaults \"other than above\" terminate only after thirty days' notice and a failure to cure. Which side of that line a given breach falls on is left to argument.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.32},
   {"issue": "Section 3(F) terminates the whole Agreement on a drilling default, whereas Section 5(B) terminates it only \"as to the Unearned Farmout Lands\" for wrongful abandonment, and Section 13(A) lumps both together as terminating the Agreement. The scope of termination cannot be settled from any one of them.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
